.Dd $Mdocdate: November 9 2026 $
.Dt IMAPIDLE.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm imapidle.conf
.Nd imapidle configuration file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa ~/.config/imapidle/imapidle.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
tells
.Sy imapidle(1)
which IMAP accounts to watch and which
.Sy mbsync
channel to run when mail changes.
The file is INI.
One section is one account.
The section name is a label used in log lines.
.Pp
The directory should be mode 0700 and the file mode 0600.
A
.Va password
value is stored in the file.
A
.Va password_cmd
value is not a place to hide a secret: anyone who can read the file can
read the command.
.Pp
Lines that start with
.Ql #
or
.Ql ;
are comments.
Text after a value is not a comment.
.Pp
A section that is missing a required key, sets both password forms, or
names a bad port is logged and skipped.
Other sections are still used.
If every section is skipped, or the file cannot be read,
.Nm imapidle
exits 1 at startup.
.Sh KEYS
.Bl -tag -width "mbsync_channel"
.It Va server
IMAP host name.
Required.
.It Va port
TCP port.
The default is 993.
.It Va user
IMAP login name.
Required.
.It Va password
The IMAP password, used as-is.
Set this or
.Va password_cmd ,
not both.
The value is not written to the log.
.It Va password_cmd
A command passed to
.Pa /bin/sh
with
.Fl c .
The first line of its standard output, without the trailing newline, is
the IMAP password.
The command must exit 0.
Empty output, a non-zero exit, or no exit within 30 seconds fails that
account only.
The command is run again on each connection, so a helper that prints a
fresh access token can be dropped in here.
OAuth itself is not implemented;
.Nm imapidle
only reads the password the command prints.
The command text and the password are not written to the log.
Standard error from the command is discarded.
.It Va mbsync_channel
Channel name passed to
.Sy mbsync .
Required.
It must match a channel in the
.Sy mbsync
configuration.
.Nm imapidle
does not read that file.
.It Va wait
Seconds to sleep inside the
.Sy mbsync
child before exec.
The default is 0.
This does not stall the IMAP loop.
.It Va folder0 , folder1 , ...
Mailbox names, starting at
.Va folder0
with no gaps.
A value is either
.Ar name
or
.Ar name;priority=N .
Any other suffix is ignored.
A missing priority is 5.
.It Va folder
Used only when no
.Va folderN
key is present.
The default mailbox is
.Cm INBOX .
.It Va priority
Used only with
.Va folder ,
not with
.Va folderN .
The default is 5.
.El
.Pp
Every folder in a section shares that section's
.Va mbsync_channel ,
.Va user ,
and password.
.Sh DEBOUNCE
Priority selects how long
.Nm imapidle
ignores later events after it has started
.Sy mbsync
for that connection:
.Bl -column "priority" "seconds" -offset indent
.It Sy priority Ta Sy seconds
.It 2 or less Ta 8
.It 3 Ta 180
.It 4 Ta 300
.It 5 Ta 420
.It 6 Ta 540
.It 7 Ta 660
.It 8 Ta 780
.It 9 or more Ta 3600
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/imapidle/imapidle.conf
Default path.
.It Pa ~/.config/imapidle/known_hosts
Certificates accepted while using the default path.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A password stored in the file, with a hot inbox and a lazy archive:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[personal]
server = imap.example.com
port = 993
user = you@example.com
password = secret
mbsync_channel = personal
folder0 = INBOX;priority=1
folder1 = Archive;priority=9
.Ed
.Pp
The same shape when a command prints the password.
.Sy pizauth show personal
is only an example of such a command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[work]
server = imap.example.com
user = you@work.example
password_cmd = pizauth show work
mbsync_channel = work
folder = INBOX
priority = 3
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr syslog 3 ,
.Sy imapidle(1)
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Todd T. Fries Aq Mt todd@fries.net
